From f43722a20a6748c8d28f720caa5ca31d7a517486 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "David C. Manuelda" <StormByte@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:59:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] avformat/mov: align durations after subtitle edit lists

When applying an edit list without CTTS, find_prev_closest_index left
the time-to-sample index at zero even when the selected sample started
later in the track. The rebuilt index then assigned samples durations
from the wrong entries, extending the final mov_text cue across the
preceding gap.

Initialize the time-to-sample cursor from the selected sample for
MOV_TEXT only. An unrestricted change also alters the duration alignment
of other codecs: fate-quickdraw loses an expected two-tick frame duration,
replacing it with one tick. Keep their existing cursor handling unchanged.

Verify the negative-start long-gap round trip with edit-list handling
enabled. This fixes duration alignment, not the pre-existing loss of the
remaining gap when an edit starts inside an empty subtitle sample; that
boundary case requires a separate change.

Signed-off-by: David C. Manuelda <StormByte@gmail.com>
---
 libavformat/mov.c                                  |  4 ++++
 tests/fate/mov.mak                                 |  2 +-
 .../ref/fate/mov-subtitle-long-gap-negative-start  | 14 ++++++--------
 3 files changed, 11 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libavformat/mov.c b/libavformat/mov.c
index 83bf9fa8d4f..6eb78818eb8 100644
--- a/libavformat/mov.c
+++ b/libavformat/mov.c
@@ -4312,6 +4312,10 @@ static int find_prev_closest_index(AVStream *st,
                 (*tts_sample)--;
             }
         }
+    } else if (st->codecpar->codec_id == AV_CODEC_ID_MOV_TEXT &&
+               tts_index && tts_sample && tts_data && *index >= 0) {
+        *tts_index = FFMIN(*index, tts_count);
+        *tts_sample = 0;
     }
 
     /* restore AVStream state*/
